zed for it but it's  also something they can't help  it works amazingly well as a queer  allegory but it was not designed to be  another really good example lord of the  rings  however much you love it j.r.r tolkien  did not  intend to write a same-sex romantic  entanglement between sam and frodo  now the movie did lean into that  bro a little bit but the original books  no he didn't  intend that to be there but it still can  be read that way things like that  i do not consider to be on the table for  queerbaiting  because they didn't mean to  and i think there's a very key  difference between  allegory and applicability  because to look at luca for a minute and  i will avoid spoilers  as much as i possibly can on this right  now i'm just going to talk pre  uh general premise in lucca  you have these undersea people that  the surface considered to be sea  monsters  luca is one of these when he comes above  ground  he looks human when he's dry so he  passes  he meets another one like him who  wants to be more involved in the surface  world  they are feared by the people on the  surface they are called  monsters it is believed that they are  dangerous  they bond very closely with each other  and they do  eventually find other people that they  would like to be more open with who they  are about but  they just fundamentally believe it isn't  safe this absolutely works  and is applicable as a queer story and  of course  the two main characters alberto and luca  these two boys have a wonderful  relationship and a wonderful bond and i  even said in my original review that you  can  read it that way but it's also  applicable to a ton of other things  you could apply that same kind of  mentality to  my gosh almost anything you could apply  it to certain kinds of  disability be on the autism spectrum  just being a certain kind of race and  possibly being  say for instance a white passing black  person  there are plenty of other things that  it's applicable to and that's not 