g these two boys  you know starting to learn and i don't  mean gay parent like oh they're going to  get on like you know  like a young crush the way lots of  heteronormative relationships are shown  in all kinds of kids media so  i talked about this just last week so  anyways  i wasn't aware of any of that  conversation and when your head's  already in that space you're gonna come  into the film  looking for how things fit into that  conversation you've already been having  now does that mean that you  automatically see things that  aren't there no but you are going to be  more sensitive to whatever is there and  possibly read more into it than was  intended but again that's not to say  that it isn't there  so like some of the things people have  brought up that i've seen  a lot of people talk about in terms of  the overall structure and the italian  setting  as a connection to call me by your name  which at this point is one of the more  prominent recent  films to feature a gay male pairing i  haven't actually seen call me by your  name i'm not sure if i ever will now  because i didn't get around to it before  uh army hammer was better known as  doing stuff army hammer seems to have  been about but anyways i don't know if  i'll ever get around to that so i can't  confirm solidly how much it does or  doesn't sort of  uh graft onto the template of call me by  your name  what i can tell you though is that there  are a number of references and  influences that this thing wears very  much on its  sleeve and that doesn't appear to be one  of them there's a poster in the  background for the italian film lestrada  and the whole vibe has  kind of a uh more laid back that is very  much in keeping with  a lot of films from that part of the  world and honestly i'm ashamed that i  missed this one  the name of the town is porto rosso  which  really i i cannot believe this was not  intentional given how much we know the  people at pixar love miyazaki  one of miyazaki's films was 